WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.900
A memory budget is an engineering contract.

2
00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:12.040
It describes how much memory the program expects to use, which parts of the program own that memory, and which conditions were considered when the limit was chosen.

3
00:00:12.140 --> 00:00:18.220
Without that contract, developers may make local decisions that work individually but fail when combined.

4
00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:28.820
Start with the major categories: RAM, stack, heap, static storage, fixed buffers, memory pools, persistent storage, and scratch space.

5
00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:40.600
In embedded or device-facing C, some memory may have special properties such as DMA capability, alignment requirements, retention behavior, or limited write endurance.

6
00:00:40.700 --> 00:00:43.500
Those regions should be named in the design.

7
00:00:43.780 --> 00:00:48.820
The budget should define maximum input sizes and message sizes before implementation.

8
00:00:48.920 --> 00:00:55.720
If a protocol field says a message can be large, the program still needs a product-level decision about what it will accept.

9
00:00:55.820 --> 00:01:03.580
Maximums should be tied to memory, timing, and operational requirements, not only to what the format can theoretically express.

10
00:01:03.860 --> 00:01:06.100
Concurrency assumptions matter.

11
00:01:06.200 --> 00:01:18.280
A system that handles one request at a time has a different memory profile from one that handles several connections, queued messages, worker tasks, or overlapping device operations.

12
00:01:18.380 --> 00:01:24.300
Per-client buffers, per-task stacks, parser state, and retry queues can add up quickly.

13
00:01:24.580 --> 00:01:27.460
A good budget leaves room for error and recovery paths.

14
00:01:27.560 --> 00:01:35.480
Low-memory behavior still needs control structures, cleanup state, diagnostic records, and sometimes enough scratch space to return a clear error.

15
00:01:35.580 --> 00:01:42.220
If the normal path uses every available byte, the failure path may be forced into unsafe shortcuts.

16
00:01:42.500 --> 00:01:45.220
Document worst-case assumptions in plain language.

17
00:01:45.320 --> 00:01:47.880
What is the largest accepted input?

18
00:01:47.980 --> 00:01:49.820
How many operations can be active?

19
00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:51.920
What stack depth is possible?

20
00:01:52.020 --> 00:01:53.700
Which buffers are shared?

21
00:01:53.800 --> 00:01:56.760
Which allocations are allowed after startup?

22
00:01:56.860 --> 00:01:59.340
What happens if persistent storage is full?

23
00:01:59.620 --> 00:02:03.060
Finally, validate the budget on realistic targets.

24
00:02:03.160 --> 00:02:12.840
Compiler options, library choices, build profiles, logging settings, interrupt behavior, and test configuration can all change actual memory use.

25
00:02:12.940 --> 00:02:20.060
Measurement does not replace design, but it catches the gap between the design and the binary that actually runs.
